{"id": 55, "ts": 1780432203.8452792, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 3, "agent": "sarah_mitchell", "message": "I\u2019m still inclined to recommend Candidate\u202fC.\u202fJames just confirmed that C \u201ckeeps calm in a crisis\u201d and \u201ccan make correct decisions quickly,\u201d which directly addresses two of our most critical criteria\u2014stress resilience and decision quality under pressure. Those abilities are essential for long\u2011distance flights where unexpected events are common.\nHowever, we must balance those strengths against the concerns already on the table: C has difficulty communicating ideas, is perceived as egocentric, and is not very willing to pursue further education. Those traits could undermine crew cooperation and adaptability, especially on international routes that demand constant coordination and openness to feedback.\nCompared with Candidate\u202fB, who also handles stress well and is very conscientious, B\u2019s record shows a tendency to be grumpy and uncooperative, which raises similar crew\u2011cooperation red flags. At the same time, B\u2019s technical strengths (weather assessment, computer skills) are valuable, but we lack concrete evidence of his decision\u2011making speed under pressure.\nGiven the new confirmation of C\u2019s crisis composure and rapid, correct decision\u2011making, I believe his overall profile still aligns best with our safety\u2011critical needs, provided we address the communication and adaptability concerns through targeted onboarding and mentorship."}
